# ⛔ WITHOUT THIS FILE, GIT STORES WHATEVER BYTES A TOOL HAPPENED TO WRITE.
# `core.autocrlf` is `false` here (global and local), so git performs NO conversion at all: an
# editor that emits CRLF permanently flips the stored file. Measured 2026-08-05 across four repos in
# one session — `agience-ember`'s working copies had flipped, which made a 945-line change read as
# 6097 lines and would have destroyed `git blame` on five files. The real edit was invisible inside
# the noise, which is the actual cost.
#
# `text=auto` makes git normalise to LF IN THE REPOSITORY on commit while leaving working copies
# alone. It is incremental — only files that are committed get normalised — so adding it does not
# rewrite history and does not require a big-bang `git add --renormalize`.
* text=auto

# Line endings that are load-bearing, not cosmetic. A shell script or a Dockerfile with CRLF fails
# inside a Linux container with an error that names the interpreter, not the line endings.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Windows-native files keep CRLF deliberately.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
